= Pattern Design and Capture Fidelity
<sec:supp_pattern_fidelity>
== Binary mask design
<binary-mask-design>
A chrome-etched binary wavelet-noise mask provides high contrast and multiscale features suitable for displacement estimation and optical-flow processing @Cook2005@Farneback2003@WANG2024102029. The fabricated chrome feature size is $1.975 thin mu upright("m")$. This way, the effective sampling of the camera converts the binary pattern into a grayscale target, with enough contrast for sub-pixel-level tracking.

Given telecentric magnification $M approx 0.185 times$, the effective object-space sampling is $ delta = p_(upright(c a m)) / M \, $ where $p_(upright(c a m))$ is sensor pixel pitch. With a $5.5 thin mu upright("m")$ monochrome sensor, $delta approx 29.7 thin mu upright("m")$. The mask sampling was chosen so that one camera pixel integrates approximately one $15$-feature binary block, since $15 times 1.975 thin mu upright("m") approx 29.6 thin mu upright("m")$.

== Pattern-generation workflow
<pattern-generation-workflow>
The mask design starts from a grayscale wavelet-noise field generated by summing band-pass noise components over multiple spatial scales. This grayscale target is converted to a binary fabrication raster by positional dithering, after which fiducial markers and alignment guides are overlaid. The result contains multi-scale texture for optical-flow tracking.

The final fabrication raster is $19260 times 19260$ pixels. Figure #ref(<fig:supp_mask_layout>, supplement: none) shows the layout representation of the fabricated mask. With a $1.975 thin mu upright("m")$ fabrication feature size, the active design spans approximately $38.0 thin upright(m m)$, around $1.5 thin upright("in")$. The effective sensor-simulation block is $15$ fabrication pixels, or $29.6 thin mu upright("m")$, which closely matches the object-space camera sampling. The binary chrome mask was fabricated by direct laser writing on a Heidelberg Instruments uPG101 system. Table #ref(<tab:supp_mask_generation>, supplement: none) summarizes the relevant mask-design parameters.

