= Image processing
<image-processing>
Each measurement uses a reference pattern image and a lens-distorted pattern image, stored as lossless TIFFs. Frame averaging reduces capture noise; single-shot acquisition is possible when the signal-to-noise ratio is sufficient. An edge detector locates the lens boundary, estimates the geometric center, and builds a circular soft mask for the region used in displacement-field retrieval. The lens image is darker than the reference image, so histogram matching is applied to the distorted capture before displacement-field retrieval @Gonzalez2018section[Sec.~3.3]. For the measurements reported here, the displacement field is initialized with the physical radial model and then refined by direct image-consistency optimization, as described in Section~#emph[Initial State Estimation of the Displacement Field]. Farnebäck optical flow was also tested as an alternative initialization. Because the measured displacement is produced by refraction through the lens, we refer to the resulting field as the distortion field.

== Computational implementation
<computational-implementation>
The reconstruction code is implemented in PyTorch. Distortion-field retrieval, differentiable ray-tracing optimization, and final surface fitting are separate modules. The surface-fitting module can also be applied to external heightmaps such as profilometric measurements. Representative single-pair spherical-lens processing runs on $950 times 950$ grids required approximately 5--11 min on one NVIDIA RTX A4500 GPU with 20 GB memory. These timings include displacement-field retrieval, staged surface optimization with heightmap refinement, and final parametric fitting. Within these runs, displacement-field computation alone required approximately 5--6 s per pair, including radial initialization and dense image-consistency refinement. Diagnostic plotting and artifact export brought the displacement module's total runtime to approximately 20--23 s.

On a server with two Intel Xeon Gold 6330 CPUs, the spherical reconstruction pipelines required approximately 5--15 min per capture pair on one NVIDIA A100 GPU with 80 GB memory, using precomputed displacement fields on $950 times 950$ grids. These wall times include preprocessing, staged optimization, final parametric fitting, and output generation. An individual exploratory AL2550 heightmap reconstruction required approximately 8.5 min on the same hardware. These timings exclude displacement-field retrieval and subsequent independent validation.

The ten-acquisition AL2550 rotational benchmark used one A100 GPU on the same server, running PyTorch 2.6 with CUDA 12.4 and double-precision reconstruction. Retrieval of the ten input displacement fields required approximately 20--50 s per field, totaling about 6 min of computation. The joint fit to all ten acquisitions required approximately 4.5 h of accumulated optimization time across five stages totaling 11,900 iterations on $950 times 950$ grids. This timing excludes displacement-field retrieval, output generation, and subsequent validation. Runtime depends on the number of acquisitions, grid size, surface representation, iteration budgets, and stopping criteria.

= Initial State Estimation of the Displacement Field
<sec:supp_initial_state>
The displacement field is initialized with a compact radial model fitted to sparse image correspondences. This model seeds the subsequent dense image-consistency refinement; the refined displacement field is then passed to the differentiable ray-tracing surface reconstruction.

== Physical model for axisymmetric lenses with a conic refractive surface
<physical-model-for-axisymmetric-lenses-with-a-conic-refractive-surface>
Consider a lens with a planar side and a conic refractive surface. Let $s \( r \) = d z \/ d r$ be the radial surface slope. Explicitly, for the conic surface without aspheric correction, the slope is $ s \( r \) = frac(r, R sqrt(1 - \( 1 + k \) r^2 \/ R^2)) . $ An axially incident ray sees a local surface tilt $ theta_s = arctan s \( r \) . $ For a single refracting interface from index $n_1$ to $n_2$, Snell's law gives the transmitted angle with respect to the surface normal as $ theta_t = arcsin #h(-1em) (n_1 / n_2 sin theta_s) . $ The ray deviation from the optical axis is $theta_s - theta_t$, and propagation over an effective distance $L$ gives $ u_r \( r \) = L tan #h(-1em) [theta_s - arcsin #h(-1em) (n_1 / n_2 sin theta_s)] . $

For initialization, the compact non-paraxial approximation is used $ u_r \( r \) approx alpha tan #h(-1em) [beta arctan s \( r \)] \, $<eq:supp_initial_tangent_model> where $alpha$ absorbs effective propagation distance and unit conversion. The factor $beta$ is set from the nominal refractive-index contrast and represents the leading angular deviation from Snell refraction. Keeping the outer tangent and the slope angle $arctan s \( r \)$ preserves the finite-angle behavior needed near the aperture edge. In the small-slope limit, Eq.~#ref(<eq:supp_initial_tangent_model>, supplement: none) reduces to $ u_r \( r \) approx alpha beta s \( r \) \, $ which is the known paraxial relationship between displacement and surface slope. The parameter $beta$ is fixed from nominal refractive indices for the lens material and surrounding medium. The parameters $alpha$, $R$, and $k$ are then fitted to the observed distortion field by least squares. This estimate provides a rapid geometric identification of the sample and can be useful during manufacturing inspection. It is nevertheless a reduced model: it assumes rotational symmetry, compresses propagation and refraction into two scalar factors, and does not account for the calibrated camera geometry or coupled multi-surface effects. We therefore use it primarily to initialize the dense displacement-field refinement. The optimized displacement field is then used by the differentiable ray-tracing reconstruction.

Figure #ref(<fig:lens_init_estimation>, supplement: none) summarizes coarse geometric estimates obtained by refitting the radial model to the refined displacement fields for selected plano-convex lenses. For lenses with nominal radius of curvature below $100 thin upright(m m)$, the radius estimate remains within about $20 %$ of the nominal value, and the center-thickness (CT) estimate remains within about $10 %$. Flatter lenses generate weaker displacement fields and show larger radial-model bias.

