= Forward Ray Model
<sec:supp_forward_ray_model>
== Camera model and non-ideal telecentricity
<camera-model-and-non-ideal-telecentricity>
The imaging model is finite-origin (pinhole-like), with optical-axis unit vector $ hat(upright(bold(u)))_(upright(a x i s)) = \( sin theta cos phi.alt \, sin theta sin phi.alt \, cos theta \) \, $ and camera center $ upright(bold(S)) = - d_(upright(o r i g i n)) thin hat(upright(bold(u)))_(upright(a x i s)) \, #h(2em) d_(upright(o r i g i n)) = W D + R_(upright(o r i g i n)) . $ Here $W D$ is the working distance (110 mm in the current setup) and $R_(upright(o r i g i n))$ is the calibrated projective ray-origin distance measured from the image plane. Figure #ref(<fig:supp_camera_projection>, supplement: none) illustrates this camera geometry.

Coordinates are expressed in millimeters in a camera-fixed world frame. The ray label $upright(bold(x))$ is the calibrated image coordinate mapped to the aperture-label plane $z = 0$, not a physical coordinate on the sensor or mask. For image column $u$ and row $v$, $upright(bold(g)) = delta \( u - u_0 \, - \( v - v_0 \) \)$, with $delta = p_(upright(c a m)) \/ M$ in millimeters per pixel; a measured flow $\( f_u \, f_v \)$ maps to $upright(bold(d))^(upright(o b s)) = delta \( f_u \, - f_v \)$. The image-row sign is reversed to make world $y$ point upward.

The local incoming direction for grid location $upright(bold(g))_(i j)^(\( 3 \)) = \( x_(i j) \, y_(i j) \, 0 \)$ is $ hat(upright(bold(v)))_(upright(i n)) \( upright(bold(g))_(i j) \) = frac(upright(bold(g))_(i j)^(\( 3 \)) - upright(bold(S)), parallel upright(bold(g))_(i j)^(\( 3 \)) - upright(bold(S)) parallel) . $

