==== Calibration vs reconstruction optimization.
<calibration-vs-reconstruction-optimization.>
The finite-origin parameter $R_(upright(o r i g i n))$ is derived from telecentricity. For the AL2550 rotational benchmark, the complete camera state, including the axis angles, is fixed; each acquisition has its own lens translation and tilt. The heightmap workflow permits residual pose refinement.

== Surface intersections and Snell refraction
<surface-intersections-and-snell-refraction>
Rays are traced through front and back interfaces. For curved interfaces, intersection is solved by Newton iterations @Gross2005. For a planar interface, a closed-form ray-plane intersection is used.

At each interface, vector Snell refraction is $ hat(upright(bold(v)))_r = n_1 / n_2 hat(upright(bold(v)))_i + (n_1 / n_2 cos theta_i - cos theta_t) hat(upright(bold(n))) \, $ with $ cos theta_i = - hat(upright(bold(v)))_i dot.op hat(upright(bold(n))) \, $ $ cos theta_t = sqrt(1 - (n_1 / n_2)^2 (1 - cos^2 theta_i)) . $ In total internal reflection, the term inside the square root becomes negative and the ray weight is set to zero.

== Reference and lensed landing positions
<reference-and-lensed-landing-positions>
For each aperture-label coordinate $upright(bold(x))$, the lensed landing point on the comparison plane is $upright(bold(P))_theta^(upright(f i n a l)) \( upright(bold(x)) \)$. The reference (no-lens) landing point is obtained by propagating the unrefracted ray to the same comparison plane $z = z_(upright(c m p))$, $ upright(bold(P))_theta^(upright(r e f)) \( upright(bold(x)) \) = upright(bold(S)) + frac(z_(upright(c m p)) - S_z, hat(v)_(upright(i n) \, z) \( upright(bold(x)) \)) hat(upright(bold(v)))_(upright(i n)) \( upright(bold(x)) \) . $ In the baseline AL2550 configuration, $z_(upright(c m p))$ is the global-$z$ plane through the posed rear vertex. In the slab-assisted configuration, it is the exit plane of the $3.181 thin upright(m m)$ slab, whose entrance is anchored at that rear-vertex height. The slab faces remain parallel to the global $x$--$y$ plane. Propagation from the tilted rear surface to the comparison or slab-entrance plane can be signed. The bare reference ray contains neither the lens nor the added slab. These planes follow the optical-stack convention used by the saved reconstruction; they need not coincide with $z = 0$.

= Predicted Displacement Calculation
<predicted-displacement-calculation>
Sensor measurements provide an observed displacement field in image coordinates. Let $upright(bold(g))_(i j)$ be the reference image-grid coordinate in aperture-label millimeters and let $upright(bold(x))$ denote a continuous lensed-image coordinate. The ray tracer computes a forward map from an aperture-label coordinate to a physical landing point on the stack-dependent comparison plane, $ cal(F)_theta \( upright(bold(x)) \) = [upright(bold(P))_theta^(upright(f i n a l)) \( upright(bold(x)) \)]^(x y) \, $ where $theta$ denotes the current lens and calibration parameters. The no-lens reference ray gives the corresponding reference landing point $[upright(bold(P))_theta^(upright(r e f)) \( upright(bold(g))_(i j) \)]^(x y)$.

This forward map is not itself the optical-flow displacement. Optical flow compares the reference and lensed images in the sensor domain: the pattern feature seen at reference coordinate $upright(bold(g))_(i j)$ appears at a lensed coordinate $upright(bold(x))_(i j)^(*)$. Therefore $upright(bold(x))_(i j)^(*)$ is defined by the inverse correspondence $ cal(F)_theta \( upright(bold(x))_(i j)^(*) \) = [upright(bold(P))_theta^(upright(r e f)) \( upright(bold(g))_(i j) \)]^(x y) . $<eq:supp_inverse_condition> The predicted sensor-domain displacement is the displacement associated with this inverse coordinate, not the raw difference of two landing points evaluated at the same grid sample.

== Inverse Correspondence for Sensor-Domain Displacement
<sec:supp_inverse_correspondence>
Define the entry displacement field $ upright(bold(Delta))_theta \( upright(bold(x)) \) = [upright(bold(P))_theta^(upright(r e f)) \( upright(bold(x)) \)]^(x y) - [upright(bold(P))_theta^(upright(f i n a l)) \( upright(bold(x)) \)]^(x y) . $<eq:supp_entry_delta> For on-axis orthographic rays, $[upright(bold(P))_theta^(upright(r e f)) \( upright(bold(x)) \)]^(x y) = upright(bold(x))$, and Eq.~#ref(<eq:supp_inverse_condition>, supplement: none) becomes the fixed-point relation $ upright(bold(x))_(i j)^(*) = upright(bold(g))_(i j) + upright(bold(Delta))_theta \( upright(bold(x))_(i j)^(*) \) . $ The iteration is damped in practice, $ upright(bold(x))^(\( n + 1 \)) = \( 1 - alpha \) upright(bold(x))^(\( n \)) + alpha [upright(bold(g))_(i j) + upright(bold(Delta))_theta \( upright(bold(x))^(\( n \)) \)] \, $ with $upright(bold(x))^(\( 0 \)) = upright(bold(g))_(i j)$. The sensor-coordinate displacement, expressed in aperture-label millimeters, is the difference between the inverse and reference coordinates: $ upright(bold(d))^(upright(p r e d)) \( upright(bold(g))_(i j) \) = upright(bold(x))_(i j)^(*) - upright(bold(g))_(i j) . $<eq:supp_predicted_displacement>

For finite-origin cameras, the reference landing point is not generally equal to the sensor-grid coordinate. Define the projective correction $ upright(bold(s))_theta \( upright(bold(x)) \) = [upright(bold(P))_theta^(upright(r e f)) \( upright(bold(x)) \)]^(x y) - upright(bold(x)) . $ Then the inverse condition becomes $ upright(bold(x))_(i j)^(*) = upright(bold(g))_(i j) + upright(bold(Delta))_theta \( upright(bold(x))_(i j)^(*) \) + [upright(bold(s))_theta \( upright(bold(g))_(i j) \) - upright(bold(s))_theta \( upright(bold(x))_(i j)^(*) \)] . $<eq:supp_projective_inverse> Thus $upright(bold(d))^(upright(p r e d)) = upright(bold(Delta))_theta \( upright(bold(x))^(*) \) + upright(bold(s))_theta \( upright(bold(g)) \) - upright(bold(s))_theta \( upright(bold(x))^(*) \)$. For tilted orthographic rays, the reference offset is constant and cancels. For finite-origin rays it varies across the field. The inverse uses 32 damped iterations with $alpha = 0.5$ and returns the sensor-coordinate displacement $upright(bold(x))^(*) - upright(bold(g))$.

==== Alternative direct correspondence loss.
<alternative-direct-correspondence-loss.>
The measured optical flow can also be used directly as a correspondence. If $upright(bold(d))^(upright(o b s)) \( upright(bold(g)) \)$ maps reference coordinates to lensed-image coordinates, then the observed lensed coordinate is $upright(bold(g)) + upright(bold(d))^(upright(o b s)) \( upright(bold(g)) \)$. A direct pattern-plane residual would enforce $ cal(F)_theta #h(-1em) (upright(bold(g)) + upright(bold(d))^(upright(o b s)) \( upright(bold(g)) \)) approx [upright(bold(P))_theta^(upright(r e f)) \( upright(bold(g)) \)]^(x y) . $<eq:supp_direct_correspondence> This avoids explicitly solving the inverse map because the measured flow supplies the correspondence. For a locally invertible forward map on the same valid support, Eq.~#ref(<eq:supp_direct_correspondence>, supplement: none) and the sensor-coordinate residual in Eq.~#ref(<eq:supp_predicted_displacement>, supplement: none) have the same zero-residual correspondence. Their least-squares objectives are generally different: locally, the pattern-plane residual is the sensor-coordinate residual multiplied by the forward-map Jacobian. Equal weighting in the two planes therefore does not give an equivalent fit in the presence of nonzero residuals.

= Reconstruction Objective and Optimization Stages
<sec:supp_reconstruction_objective>
== Loss
<loss>
The reconstruction objective is $ cal(L) = cal(L)_(upright(d a t a)) + cal(L)_(upright(p e n a l t y)) \, $ where the data term compares predicted and observed displacement on the fixed valid support and $ cal(L)_(upright(p e n a l t y)) = sum_k lambda_k cal(P)_k . $ For the AL2550 benchmark, the data term is the component-wise squared L2 objective used by the reconstruction code. For measurement $m$ and component $c in { x \, y }$, let $e_(m \, c) = d_(m \, c)^(upright(p r e d)) - d_(m \, c)^(upright(o b s))$ and let $q_(m \, c)^99$ denote the 99th-percentile clamp applied to the component-wise squared residual field before support averaging. The reported objective is $ cal(L)_(upright(d a t a)) = frac(1, sum_m w_m) sum_m w_m 1 / 2 sum_(c in { x \, y }) "mean"_(cal(S)_m) [min #h(-1em) (e_(m \, c)^2 \, q_(m \, c)^99)] \, $<eq:supp_al2550_data_loss> with fixed support $cal(S)_m$ for each measurement. The same objective is used for the additional-optimization and displacement-scale tests in Section #ref(<sec:supp_al2550_validation>, supplement: none).

For the general reconstruction pipeline, geometric penalties can be enabled for quantities such as center thickness and edge thickness. In the AL2550 additional-optimization tests discussed below, the lens thickness is fixed and no center-thickness or edge-thickness penalties are active.

== Three-stage protocol
<three-stage-protocol>
==== Stage 1: conic-only fit.
<stage-1-conic-only-fit.>
The first stage excludes aspheric coefficients and uses $ z \( r \) = frac(r^2, R [1 + sqrt(1 - \( 1 + k \) r^2 / R^2)]) . $

==== Stage 2: heightmap refinement.
<stage-2-heightmap-refinement.>
After stage-1 convergence, the single-sided spherical measurements refine the front-surface heightmap and hold the back surface at its fitted parametric shape. Both refracting surfaces contribute to the predicted displacement. Paired front/back measurements can refine two heightmaps. Smoothness regularization remains weak; the data term dominates.

Refinement selects the evaluated state with the lowest data-plus-smoothness objective among states with valid physical ray intersections and stable correspondence inversion. In the fully weighted interior of the finite measurement mask, 32- and 64-step damped inversions must close within $0.01 thin mu upright(m)$ and their queries must agree within $0.01 thin mu upright(m)$. A positive-definite symmetric Jacobian of the bilinear landing map on a convex disk containing all queries supplies a sufficient injectivity check. These numerical checks determine which evaluated states are eligible for restoration. The exported solution is also checked over the full modeled physical ray-label domain. The checks establish numerical consistency, not measurement accuracy.

==== Stage 3: final parametric fitting.
<stage-3-final-parametric-fitting.>
The optimized heightmap is reduced to optical shape parameters by fitting a posed parametric surface. The fit uses finite points within the fixed circular aperture in measurement coordinates, with support held constant during pose optimization. This registration step separates surface form from residual translation and orientation, as required for dense 3D measurements without an independent datum @MCBRIDE1996597@HILL2002457@elhayek2014@El-Hayek2015-qe. The fit estimates vertex-coordinate offsets $\( Delta x \, Delta y \, Delta z \)$, measured with respect to the reconstruction coordinate origin, and angular pose $\( alpha \, beta \)$. In the implemented routine, the posed surface is obtained from the local sag surface using $R_(upright(p o s e)) = R_z \( alpha \) R_y \( beta \)$. Here, $beta$ represents the tilt of the surface axis away from the reconstruction $z$-axis. The angle $alpha$ specifies the azimuthal direction of that tilt in the $x$-$y$ plane.

After this registration, the local sag follows the ISO 10110-12 asphere convention @ISO10110-12-2019: $ z \( r \) = frac(r^2, R [1 + sqrt(1 - \( 1 + k \) r^2 / R^2)]) + sum_(i = 1)^N A_(2 i) r^(2 i) \, $ with fitted parameter vector $ Theta_(upright(f i t)) = { R \, k \, A_(2 i) \, alpha \, beta \, Delta x \, Delta y \, Delta z } \, $ obtained by minimizing vertical residuals between the recovered heightmap and the posed model. The optimization uses Adam followed by L-BFGS refinement @kingma2017adam@Liu1989. For spherical analysis, $k$ and $A_(2 i)$ are fixed to zero; for aspheric analysis, they are estimated after the heightmap stage, up to $A_10$ in the present implementation. Including $\( alpha \, beta \)$ in the fit prevents residual surface tilt from being absorbed into curvature or aspheric coefficients.

The conic--heightmap--parametric sequence above describes the general reconstruction and the heightmap-based residual analyses presented in this Supplement. For the quantitative AL2550 benchmark reported in the main text and in Section #ref(<sec:supp_al2550_validation>, supplement: none), a shared normalized rotational-asphere model with active $R \, k \, A_4 \, A_6 \, A_8$ and fixed $A_10 = 0$ is optimized directly in staged pose, radius, conic, and aspheric refinements on a fixed physical aperture. This keeps the surface comparison tied directly to the rotational-asphere parameters used in the forward model. The AL2550 stages use Adam with a reduce-on-plateau schedule and restore the best state of each stage.

The AL2550 acquisition used the Grasshopper3 camera in Mono16 mode, with 10 effective ADC bits and unsigned 16-bit TIFF storage. Ten accepted frames were averaged with floating-point accumulation for each reference and measurement burst, and each mean was stored as a float32 TIFF. Each physical placement combines two undisturbed repeat bursts; their means are averaged arithmetically before one flow estimate is computed. They share one pose. Seven baseline placements and three slab-assisted placements have equal measurement weight. Each uses its capture-associated reference burst mean; closing reference bursts are saved for drift diagnostics. The fixed $950 times 950$ flow crop is sensor-centered. Flow is estimated on a 405-pixel-radius mask, then scored on a fixed $20 thin upright(m m)$-diameter disk centered on the paired fiducial-derived carrier center estimate and intersected with valid observed support.

The flow pair is jointly scaled to uint8 and histogram-matched. A physical radial fit initializes dense brightness refinement with data/smoothness/curl weights $10 \/ 0.01 \/ 0.01$. Adam uses at most 10,000 iterations, initial learning rate 0.005, plateau patience 50, linear learning-rate reduction 0.01 to a minimum $10^(- 7)$, and early stopping after 100 iterations without improvement greater than $10^(- 7)$. A strong-Wolfe L-BFGS tail has a 100-iteration maximum. No preblur or postfilter smoothing is enabled. Local displacement outliers use a 51-pixel window and 2.5-standard-deviation threshold.

Shared shape starts at $R = 25.56 thin upright(m m)$, $k = A_4 = A_6 = A_8 = A_10 = 0$, with no random initialization jitter. Initial acquisition poses are restored from the preceding pose-only bootstrap; configured axial rotations remain fixed. Lateral translation and two lens tilt angles remain active at every stage; axial translation, axial rotation, rear-plane shape, thickness, refractive index, and camera state are fixed. Thickness and edge-thickness penalties are disabled.

The five Adam stages are pose only (2500 iterations), radius and pose (1200), conic and pose (1200), and two all-active asphere/pose stages (4000 and 3000). All saved stages completed their budgets. The respective no-improvement stopping patiences are 600, 500, 500, 600, and 600 iterations, with zero minimum improvement. Reduce-on-plateau halves the learning rate after 200, 200, 200, 250, and 300 stagnant iterations, with relative threshold $10^(- 7)$ and minimum learning rate $10^(- 9)$. Warmup applies to the first and final two stages (300, 100, and 100 steps). Shape and pose coordinates have separate learning rates recorded in the frozen configuration. Each stage restores the state with the lowest optical objective; CCP agreement is evaluated after reconstruction and does not enter the loss or state selection.

The frozen AL2550 camera uses $theta approx 0.77^compose$, $phi.alt approx 90.8^compose$, telecentricity parameter $0.150^compose$, $W D = 110 thin upright(m m)$, and a $22 thin upright(m m)$ calibration diameter. The corresponding distances are $d_(upright(o r i g i n)) approx 4.21 thin upright(m)$ and $R_(upright(o r i g i n)) approx 4.10 thin upright(m)$. These values are rounded for presentation; the reconstruction configuration stores the full numerical state. Here $theta$ is measured from $+ z$ and $phi.alt$ from $+ x$ toward $+ y$\; lens pose uses $R_z R_y R_x$. This camera configuration is held fixed during the AL2550 inverse. The calibration table separately summarizes the repeated slab fits.

= Residual and Aberration Analysis
<sec:supp_residual_analysis>
The optimized heightmap contains spatial information from the data-consistency term. Conventional geometric parameters are obtained only in the subsequent fitting step. After final fitting, residuals are evaluated in the fitted local surface coordinates and inside the clear-aperture mask. Waviness is separated from high-frequency content with an isotropic Gaussian filter @ISO2025. The Gaussian low-pass convention has 50% amplitude transmission at the stated cutoff wavelength $lambda_c$, with $sigma = lambda_c sqrt(ln 2) \/ \( sqrt(2) pi \)$ in physical coordinates. The example below uses $lambda_c = 1.5 thin upright(m m)$ to suppress shorter-period content before Zernike analysis; the cutoff is a processing choice, not a demonstrated optical-performance boundary. The filtered residual is decomposed into Zernike modes to summarize the dominant aberration-like departures @Mahajan2007Zernike.

We consider two residual definitions. The first compares the optimized heightmap $Z_(upright(h m))$ with its best-fitting parametric surface $Z_(upright(f i t))$: $ Delta Z_(upright(f i t)) \( upright(bold(x)) \) = Z_(upright(h m)) \( upright(bold(x)) \) - Z_(upright(f i t)) \( upright(bold(x)) \) . $ This residual isolates departures that remain after removing the best-fit global shape and is therefore suited to analyzing local surface structure. The second compares the optimized heightmap with the nominal design surface $Z_(upright(d e s i g n))$: $ Delta Z_(upright(d e s i g n)) \( upright(bold(x)) \) = Z_(upright(h m)) \( upright(bold(x)) \) - Z_(upright(d e s i g n)) \( upright(bold(x)) \) . $ This residual includes both local surface departures and global parameter deviations from design. Either residual can be converted to wavefront error before Zernike decomposition: $ W \( upright(bold(x)) \) = frac(n - 1, lambda) Delta Z \( upright(bold(x)) \) \, $ Here $W$ is in waves, $n$ is dimensionless, and $Delta Z$ and $lambda$ must use the same length units. This is a thin-phase, near-normal-incidence optical-path approximation for a sag departure replacing air with lens material. It neglects oblique propagation, coupled departures of both surfaces, and downstream wavefront propagation. We use $lambda = 633 thin upright(n m)$ as an analysis reference; the AL2550 residual figure uses the fixed index $n = 1.518$, so its wave conversion is illustrative and is separate from the 565-nm reconstruction. A wavelength-specific index is required for a dispersive optical prediction. The interpretation depends on the residual definition. $Delta Z_(upright(f i t))$ emphasizes residual form error after removing the best-fitting posed surface. $Delta Z_(upright(d e s i g n))$ measures departure from the design prescription and therefore includes parameter bias, including residual power error. In the present analysis, the residual is fitted with 15 Zernike terms, which is sufficient for the low-order content shown here. The reported high-order reconstruction excludes the first four Noll terms, corresponding to piston, tip/tilt, and defocus, and therefore emphasizes astigmatism, coma, and spherical-aberration-like terms. Higher-order expansions can be used when the residual contains more complex structure @Rimmer:75@Svechnikov:15. Figure #ref(<fig:heightmap_residual_zernike_analysis>, supplement: none) reports $Delta Z_(upright(f i t))$ for the LA1608-A example. The design-referenced residual is omitted for compactness.

