We also show an exploratory residual-structure comparison to illustrate the type of spatial information captured by the heightmap reconstruction. This comparison is deliberately kept separate from the quantitative AL2550 benchmark above. The quantitative AL2550 result uses a direct rotational-asphere reconstruction. The residual maps below come from the more flexible heightmap reconstruction, for which pose--form separation and modal amplitudes have not been validated to the same level. Each dataset is randomly subsampled to 100,000 valid height points (seed 2026) and cropped to $14 times 14 thin upright(m m)$ before fitting and subtracting a second-order 2D polynomial. The nominal aspheric profile is not used for this subtraction. This subtraction removes quadratic structure, including curvature and astigmatism-like terms, regardless of whether it originates in the lens, alignment, or instrument. It does not identify the removed terms as CCP error or the remaining structure as intrinsic lens waviness. The residual is then filtered with a 0.8 mm Gaussian cutoff and converted to wavefront error at $lambda = 633 thin upright(n m)$. The usable CCP field limits the comparison to a circular $14 thin upright(m m)$ window. Figure #ref(<fig:al2550_residual_zernike_comparison>, supplement: none) shows that the dominant DRT residual resembles the low-order CCP structure, particularly in the spherical Z11 aberration. Rounded to two significant figures, the Z11 coefficients are 0.45 waves for CCP and 0.61 waves for DRT; the Z5+ coefficient RMS values are 0.55 and 0.61 waves, respectively. These modal summaries are larger for DRT and do not support attenuation of its overall residual amplitude. The maps use independent color limits, so color intensity cannot be compared directly across rows. The coefficient charts share a common vertical scale. At this stage, the apparent modal resemblance is treated only as a qualitative observation. In particular, pose--surface coupling, image-calibration residuals, and representation-dependent smoothing can all alter the recovered residual field. We therefore do not use the Zernike coefficients or the DRT--CCP residual resemblance as a quantitative surface-accuracy validation. Higher camera resolution and improved image-distortion calibration should make this comparison more quantitative.

The aperture limit in Fig. #ref(<fig:al2550_residual_zernike_comparison>, supplement: none) comes from the CCP benchmark, not from the transmission geometry itself. The transmission setup is also not constrained by probe standoff in the same way as CCP; in the present instrument, the measurement distance is set by the telecentric working distance, $W D = 110 thin upright(m m)$. DRT can use a wider portion of the lens aperture because stronger curvature produces a larger displacement signal and improves conditioning relative to flatter surfaces. CCP and interferometric measurements face the opposite trend: steep slopes reduce the accessible aperture. The advantage has a boundary. Once the refracted rays exceed the angular acceptance of the telecentric imaging system, tracking and reconstruction become unreliable. This limit is related to the telecentricity and ray-acceptance constraints discussed for vision-ray metrology by Ramirez-Andrade and Falaggis @ramirez2024.

