= Imaging-System Calibration
<sec:supp_camera_calibration>
Imaging-system calibration uses the same differential measurement principle as lens reconstruction. Conventional camera calibration estimates pose and distortion from planar targets observed over several views @Zhang2000\; telecentric variants usually keep that target-based structure and replace pinhole projection with orthographic or near-orthographic models @LI2013538@Duan:22. Here, the patterned mask remains the target, but the lens sample is replaced by a commercial glass slab of known thickness and measured refractive index. The slab distorts the pattern behind it in a predictable way. Because the displacement is small, the dense Farnebäck optical-flow estimate is sufficient. The differentiable ray-tracing model is then fit to this field to estimate the angular pose of the imaging axis and the finite-origin parameter used for non-ideal telecentricity.

We represent the near-telecentric camera with a finite-origin projective model @Hartley2003[Chap.~6]. The sensor is located at working distance $W D$ from the pattern plane, and rays are traced from an effective origin behind the sensor along the calibrated optical axis; in the far-origin limit, this model approaches telecentric projection. Recent telecentric calibration work explicitly models imaging errors and resolves pose-sign ambiguity using constrained joint optimization @ZONG2026109854\; that work does not establish the finite-origin model used here. The calibrated quantities are the angular pose of the telecentric axis, parameterized by $theta$ and $phi.alt$, and the projective ray-origin distance. From this finite-origin geometry we report the derived maximum chief-ray angle across the aperture as the telecentricity error. Image coordinates are transformed using the calibrated magnification factor $M = 0.185 times$, taken from the manufacturer technical report.

Experimentally, we capture the usual reference image of the undistorted pattern and a second image with flat commercial glass slabs placed on the pattern. For an ideally telecentric system normal to the pattern plane, a flat slab would produce no lateral distortion after registration. The measured displacement field therefore constrains the angular pose and finite-origin parameter directly. Once estimated, these parameters are reused as long as the relative camera-pattern geometry remains fixed. Conversely, if the camera pose is fixed and the slab thickness is known, the same refractive calibration experiment could be used to estimate the refractive index of an unknown transparent material at the measurement wavelength.

A single-pixel sensitivity estimate follows by assuming that the smallest reliable displacement is one tenth of a camera pixel: $ theta_(upright(m i n)) approx frac(0.1 p, (1 - n_(upright(a i r)) \/ n_(upright(s l a b))) M t) \, $<eq:min_detectable_angle> where $p$ is the camera pixel pitch, $M$ is the telecentric magnification, $n_(upright(a i r))$ and $n_(upright(s l a b))$ are the refractive indices of air and the calibration slab, and $t$ is the slab thickness. Using $p = 5.5 thin mu upright(m)$, $M = 0.184$, $n_(upright(a i r)) = 1.0003$, $n_(upright(s l a b)) approx 1.52$, and $t = 10 thin upright(m m)$ gives $theta_(upright(m i n)) approx 0.05^compose$.

The estimate is conservative because it treats the measurement as if it depended on one local displacement value. In the calibration fit, the parameters are inferred from a dense displacement field over the full aperture; coherent angular changes are constrained by many pixels at once, so the effective repeatability can be better than the single-pixel estimate.

The calibration summary uses four independent slab captures. The calibration coordinate frame traces rays toward negative $z$, so the reported polar angle near $179.23^compose$ corresponds to a tilt of about $0.77^compose$ from the pattern normal. Reflecting the $z$ axis gives the forward-model convention $theta_(+ z) = 180^compose - theta_(- z)$\; the azimuth is unchanged by this reflection. Each capture was optimized separately; the table summarizes their fitted values. The frozen AL2550 camera state is specified separately in Section #ref(<sec:supp_reconstruction_objective>, supplement: none). Figure #ref(<fig:calibration_convergence>, supplement: none) illustrates convergence of the polar angle $theta$, azimuthal angle $phi.alt$, and derived telecentricity angle for a separate series of four slab fits. Their terminal telecentricity values span approximately $0.011^compose$\; the plotted envelope uses the fits available at each iteration.

