For the measurements reported in the main manuscript, calibration used two 6.5 mm commercial glass slabs stacked on the pattern to increase the signal-to-noise ratio of the retrieved displacement field. Because this material was not compatible with nominal fused quartz or fused silica, its refractive index was measured by spectroscopic ellipsometry and used empirically in the calibration model. The value used in the calibration was $n = 1.4822$ at 565 nm; measurement details are given in Section~#emph[Experimental Refractive-Index Measurements].

Figure #ref(<fig:system_calibration>, supplement: none) shows one calibration measurement, and Table #ref(<tab:angular_measurements>, supplement: none) summarizes four independent repeats. The fitted alignment is stable across repeats and shows a small measurable departure from ideal telecentricity. The maximum chief-ray angle fitted by the model is approximately $0.144^compose$. This value is the effective telecentricity error of the projective camera model, not a direct remeasurement of the manufacturer's telecentric-lens specification. The effective origin lies approximately $4.27 thin upright(m)$ behind the sensor and $4.38 thin upright(m)$ from the pattern plane, including the $110 thin upright(m m)$ working distance. The residual displacement map in Fig. #ref(<fig:system_calibration>, supplement: none) contains localized mid-spatial-frequency structure from the slab measurement and from the smoothing behavior of the Farnebäck optical-flow estimate. A global pose and finite-origin model cannot reproduce all of this structure, but the overall displacement field is fitted with a sub-micrometer RMS residual.

