The present calibration does not yet include full field-dependent image-distortion correction. Its contribution to the reported geometric errors has not been isolated. Unmodeled image distortion can affect both fitted surface parameters and residual-based aberration estimates, so agreement with catalog or CCP surfaces does not establish a bound on this contribution. The dense-field measurement offers a direct path to such a correction: after fitting the known slab refraction and global pose, repeatable residual displacement can be parameterized as imaging distortion. This extension is especially relevant given the close link between telecentricity, wavefront quality, and low-uncertainty optical metrology @Xu:2019.

= Experimental Refractive-Index Measurements
<sec:supp_refractive_index_measurements>
The refractive indices of the transparent slabs used in calibration and two-position measurements were measured by spectroscopic ellipsometry; nominal material values were not used. Measurements were performed with a Woollam M-2000 variable-angle spectroscopic ellipsometer, and the values used in the reconstruction were obtained by interpolating the mean measured dispersion curves at the illumination wavelength, $565 thin upright(n m)$. Figure #ref(<fig:refractive_index_measurements>, supplement: none) shows the measured dispersion curves and their min--max bands.

The camera-calibration measurements used two $6.5 thin upright(m m)$ commercial glass slabs stacked on the mask. These slabs were treated as commercial glass after their measured dispersion was found to be incompatible with nominal fused quartz or fused silica. The calibration value was $n = 1.4822$ at $565 thin upright(n m)$. The same material-characterization procedure was also applied to a $0.991 thin upright(m m)$ soda-lime microscope slide and a $3.181 thin upright(m m)$ fused-silica slab (Chemglass Life Sciences CQG-0620-05), which were tested as intermediate layers for the two-position measurement scheme. Interpolation at $565 thin upright(n m)$ gave $n = 1.5137$ for the soda-lime glass and $n = 1.4588$ for the fused silica. The narrow min--max bands indicate low measurement-to-measurement variation in the fitted refractive index.

