= Measurement Diversity and Conditioning
<sec:supp_conditioning_strategies>
Transmission inverse problems can be ill-conditioned because coupled surface and pose changes produce similar transmitted-ray displacements. For AL2550, the rear surface is planar and held known, so the relevant ambiguity is between the correlated rotational-asphere parameters and acquisition pose over the finite aperture. Multiple rotations, reseats, and slab-assisted acquisitions provide complementary sampling and optical sensitivity. In-plane rotation alone does not change the intrinsic shape of a rotational surface; differences between these acquisitions arise from placement, sampling, and optical configuration.

The data contain seven baseline acquisitions (side A) and three slab-assisted acquisitions (side B), with a calibrated $3.181 thin upright(m m)$ fused-silica slab of refractive index 1.4588 inserted between mask and lens. Both sides include nominal $0^compose$, $45^compose$, and $90^compose$ states; side A also includes four nominal-zero reseats.

A UV-laser-cut transparent tracking ring with coded fiducials provides carrier center estimates and available in-plane orientation measurements. The fixed $20 thin upright(m m)$-diameter scoring disks use paired fiducial-derived carrier centers, distinct from the detected physical-boundary overlays in Fig. #ref(<fig:supp_fiducial_rotation_states>, supplement: none). Tracking angles are available for eight acquisitions; the rotational reconstruction retains nominal in-plane angles for all ten. Lateral decenter and two-axis tilt are fitted independently and are not forced to the tracking centers. Section #ref(<sec:supp_center_sensitivity>, supplement: none) assesses sensitivity to replacing the scoring centers with boundary-derived estimates.

