= CCP Benchmark Processing
<sec:supp_ccp_processing>
The lens was measured with a Nanovea ST400 non-contact point-scanning profilometer @nanovea@Blateyron2011@hao2023. The archived AL2550 XYZ export covers approximately $22 times 22 thin upright(m m)$ with $10 thin mu upright(m)$ lateral sampling and was produced using the thresholded export from the original profilometer processing. Figure #ref(<fig:supp_ccp_3d_view>, supplement: none) shows a three-dimensional view of the measured surface. The lateral calibration factors applied to the exported coordinates are 1.0127 and 1.0135 in the two scan directions. The total AL2550 sag is approximately $3.1 thin upright(m m)$, larger than the $1.1 thin upright(m m)$ probe range, so the outer steep region is not used as the quantitative validation aperture. The measurable slope is also limited by the objective numerical aperture @Blateyron2011@nanovea.

For the benchmark reported in the main text, the CCP validation support is frozen independently of the fitted surface. The aperture center is estimated from the measured apex, after which a fixed $14 thin upright(m m)$-diameter circular window is selected in the CCP measurement frame. From the archived XYZ export onward, finite samples inside this window are included; no additional fit-dependent point rejection, interpolation, replacement, or iterative support refinement is applied. The same frozen point set is then reused for all catalog, free-asphere, and DRT comparisons. This avoids the support changes that can otherwise occur when the aperture is repeatedly re-centered on a fitted surface.

The manufacturer prescription is first registered to the CCP data with a five-degree-of-freedom rigid alignment. A separate rotational-asphere fit is then performed on the same fixed support with that alignment held fixed. Its free shape parameters are $R$, $k$, $A_4$, $A_6$, $A_8$, and $A_10$. The fit is initialized without the manufacturer aspheric coefficients. The CCP measurement is therefore an independent data source, while the rigid alignment used for the rotational fit is inherited from the catalog-registration step. Individual polynomial coefficients are not used as the primary benchmark because the finite-aperture parameterization is strongly correlated.

For two rotational sag profiles $z_1 \( r \)$ and $z_2 \( r \)$ over an aperture of radius $a$, we report the circular-area RMS difference $ sigma_(z \, A) = [2 / a^2 integral_0^a (z_1 \( r \) - z_2 \( r \))^2 r thin d r]^(1 \/ 2) . $<eq:supp_area_weighted_rms> The factor $r$ weights each radial interval by annular area, so this quantity is the RMS sag difference over the full circular aperture under rotational symmetry, not an unweighted RMS over uniformly sampled radial coordinates.

The raw CCP point residual to the rigidly aligned manufacturer surface is $1.21 thin mu upright(m)$ RMS over the fixed support. This quantity includes non-axisymmetric/local structure and measurement residuals. For comparison with the rotational DRT model, we therefore use the circular-area RMS sag difference defined in Eq.~#ref(<eq:supp_area_weighted_rms>, supplement: none). The fitted CCP rotational surface differs from the manufacturer prescription by $0.09 thin mu upright(m)$ RMS over the central $14 thin upright(m m)$ diameter. The selected DRT surface differs from the manufacturer surface by $0.50 thin mu upright(m)$ RMS and from the CCP rotational surface by $0.44 thin mu upright(m)$ RMS over the same comparison region.

