= AL2550 Reconstruction Validation and Systematic Controls
<sec:supp_al2550_validation>
The DRT reconstruction is optimized on fixed $20 thin upright(m m)$-diameter scoring disks centered on paired fiducial-derived carrier estimates and intersected with valid observed support. These disks remain fixed while lateral decenter and tilt are fitted independently. The joint reconstruction contains seven baseline measurements and three slab-assisted measurements, with a separate acquisition pose for each measurement and one shared rotational lens surface. Side B includes the measured fused-quartz slab in the forward model. The central $14 thin upright(m m)$ region is used only when comparing the resulting rotational surface with CCP; CCP is never used in the reconstruction objective.

== Stagewise surface trajectory
<stagewise-surface-trajectory>
Figure #ref(<fig:supp_al2550_stagewise_ccp>, supplement: none) shows the optical loss and the circular-area surface difference to the independently acquired CCP rotational surface at initialization and after each reconstruction stage. Pose adjustment reduces the optical loss without changing the initialized surface; subsequent shape optimization reduces both quantities through the final reconstruction reported in the main text.

#figure(image("source/supplement/figs/AL2550_stagewise_ccp_rms_corrected.pdf", width: 80.0%),
  caption: [
    AL2550 reconstruction trajectory from initialization through the completed optimization stages. Blue circles, left axis: circular-area RMS sag difference relative to the independent CCP rotational fit over the common central $14 thin upright(m m)$-diameter aperture, evaluated using Eq.~#ref(<eq:supp_area_weighted_rms>, supplement: none). Orange squares, right axis: optical loss from fitting the measured displacement fields. Both quantities share the stage axis. The initialization and pose-adjusted states have identical surface sag because the first stage optimizes acquisition poses only. CCP is not part of the reconstruction objective.
  ]
)
<fig:supp_al2550_stagewise_ccp>

== Scoring-center sensitivity
<sec:supp_center_sensitivity>
We compare the reported fiducial-derived scoring centers with centers obtained by averaging physical-boundary detections from the two replicate images. Two matched 2000-iteration joint continuations start from the same final shape and poses, using identical frozen displacement fields and optimization settings; only the fixed scoring centers differ. The recovered surfaces differ by $0.0088 thin mu upright(m)$ area RMS and at most $0.0213 thin mu upright(m)$ over the central $14 thin upright(m m)$ diameter. Their RMS differences from the CCP rotational reference are $0.521 thin mu upright(m)$ for fiducial centers and $0.518 thin mu upright(m)$ for boundary centers. Both exceed the reported reconstruction's $0.435 thin mu upright(m)$ despite lower optical loss, separating the small center effect from the larger continuation drift. The reported reconstruction is retained.

== Additional optimization and parameter identifiability
<additional-optimization-and-parameter-identifiability>
To test whether the reported reconstruction is limited by incomplete convergence, 2000 additional iterations are run from the exact final state with three parameter sets: pose only, lens shape only, and joint pose plus lens shape. Pose-only optimization reduces the objective by 0.11% and leaves the sag unchanged. Shape-only optimization reduces the objective by 1.3% but changes the recovered sag by $0.40 thin mu upright(m)$ RMS and increases the CCP difference from $0.44$ to $0.52 thin mu upright(m)$. Joint pose--shape optimization gives the same surface behavior, with a 1.5% objective reduction and a $0.40 thin mu upright(m)$ RMS sag change. Thus, additional minimization of the displacement objective does not necessarily improve the recovered surface.

Independent optimization using only the baseline measurements or only the slab-assisted measurements changes the sag in almost the same radial direction. The corresponding RMS sag changes are $0.35$ and $0.41 thin mu upright(m)$, and the two radial changes are nearly collinear. The slab-assisted measurements give a larger reduction of their displacement objective, but the common sag direction shows that the bias is not specific to the slab-assisted geometry.

For the complete ten-measurement reconstruction, the normalized shape normal matrix is strongly ill-conditioned, with a positive-spectrum condition number of approximately $5 times 10^10$. Its weakest modes are dominated by the normalized $A_6$ and $A_8$ coordinates. Repeating the additional optimization with $A_6$ and $A_8$ fixed and only $R$, $k$, and $A_4$ active produces essentially the same sag change while achieving about 98% of the objective reduction. The same low-order sag direction is obtained from the baseline-only and slab-assisted-only measurements. The observed sag bias therefore does not require the weakly constrained high-order coefficients.

== Effective displacement-scale diagnostic
<effective-displacement-scale-diagnostic>
We next profile a multiplicative scale applied only to the frozen measured displacement field $upright(bold(d))^(upright(o b s))$, keeping the spatial grid, reconstruction aperture, lens state, poses, camera model, and slab model fixed. The profile uses the exact reconstruction objective (component-wise L2 with the reported 99th-percentile clamp). The best shared scale corresponds to a +0.16% displacement change and reduces the ten-measurement objective by 0.29% without changing the surface. Separate diagnostic profiles give +0.067% for side A and +0.24% for side B.

The shared factor is then applied to the frozen displacement fields and the $R \, k \, A_4$-only optimization is repeated from the same reported reconstruction. The scale-only objective and the initial objective of this run agree to numerical precision, confirming the intended initial state. With the displacement-scale adjustment, the subsequent low-order sag change decreases from $0.40$ to $0.10 thin mu upright(m)$ RMS, a 75% reduction. The final surface also agrees more closely with the independent references than the selected reconstruction: the RMS difference to CCP decreases from $0.44$ to $0.37 thin mu upright(m)$, and the difference to the manufacturer prescription decreases from $0.50$ to $0.43 thin mu upright(m)$. Figure #ref(<fig:supp_al2550_scale_profile>, supplement: none) compares the radial sag differences relative to both independent references.

#figure(image("source/supplement/figs/AL2550_scale_causal_surface_rms_corrected.pdf", width: 92.0%),
  caption: [
    Radial sag difference relative to the CCP rotational fit (left) and manufacturer prescription (right) for the selected reconstruction, an extended low-order shape optimization, and the same extended optimization after profiling a shared multiplicative displacement-scale factor. Further shape optimization alone reduces the optical objective but drives the recovered surface away from both independent references. Fitting the displacement-scale factor suppresses most of this additional sag change. The scale factor is used only as a diagnostic and is not interpreted as a calibrated correction to the optical-flow measurement.
  ]
)
<fig:supp_al2550_scale_profile>

This experiment identifies an effective displacement-scale mode that is causally coupled to the recovered low-order surface. It does not by itself identify the physical source of that mode. A multiplicative displacement change can represent optical-flow scale, spatial calibration, camera geometry, or another forward-model mismatch. The scale factor is therefore used only to diagnose sensitivity to displacement calibration and is not applied as a calibrated correction to the measurement system.

= Synthetic Surface Recoverability Controls
<sec:supp_synthetic_recoverability>
The AL2550 validation above concerns the rotational asphere recovered from measured displacement fields. Separate synthetic controls were used to determine which spatial-frequency and amplitude ranges are recoverable by the geometric inverse and by the complete image-processing chain. These controls quantify synthetic recoverability and are not experimental resolution measurements. The synthetic aperture has a $25 thin upright(m m)$ diameter; $f$ cycles per aperture denotes a directional sinusoid with spatial frequency $f \/ 25 thin upright(m m)^(- 1)$, independent of orientation. Thus the tested frequencies are 0.08, 0.16, 0.32, and $0.48 thin upright(m m)^(- 1)$. Surface metrics use a separate $301 times 301$ evaluation grid over 95% of the $12.5 thin upright(m m)$ radius; the representative ray-only surface map shows the full aperture. The sampled injected truth uses a $1025 times 1025$ heightmap. These apertures differ from the $20 thin upright(m m)$ real-data reconstruction and $14 thin upright(m m)$ rotational comparison.

The injected perturbations are directional sinusoidal sag fields spanning 2, 4, 8, and 12 cycles per aperture, with both $0^compose$ and $45^compose$ orientations and sine/cosine phase. Their RMS sag is defined over the circular aperture after removal of the mean component. Figure #ref(<fig:supp_recoverability_modes>, supplement: none) illustrates the spatial-frequency family used in the controlled studies.

#figure(image("source/supplement/figs/AL2550_sinusoidal_surface_examples.pdf", width: 80.0%),
  caption: [
    Representative directional sinusoidal surface perturbations used in the synthetic recoverability controls. The curves show normalized sag profiles across the aperture for 2, 4, 8, and 12 cycles per aperture.
  ]
)
<fig:supp_recoverability_modes>

== Ray-tracing surface recovery
<ray-tracing-surface-recovery>
The first synthetic test isolates the ray-tracing inverse before image formation and optical-flow estimation. Six RMS sag amplitudes, $0.1$, $0.25$, $0.5$, $1$, $2$, and $5 thin mu upright(m)$, were evaluated at each spatial frequency. For each frequency--amplitude pair, the four orientation/phase combinations were propagated through the AL2550 ray model for three fixed acquisition poses. Recovery used a 16-mode Cartesian-frequency basis containing all tested sinusoidal modes, and the acquisition pose $\( t_x \, t_y \, r_x \, r_y \)$ was optimized for each synthetic measurement. This test therefore includes modal cross-coupling and pose uncertainty within the ray-tracing inverse.

For truth and recovered fields $t$ and $r$ on common support, recovery gain is $chevron.l t \, r chevron.r \/ chevron.l t \, t chevron.r$, correlation is the centered Pearson correlation, and NRMSE is $upright(R M S) \( r - t \) \/ upright(R M S) \( t \)$. For the ray-only modal inverse, gain is the recovered injected-mode coefficient divided by its injected amplitude; crosstalk is the largest absolute off-diagonal coefficient ratio. Passing requires gain in $\[ 0.90 \, 1.10 \]$, crosstalk $lt.eq 0.10$, surface NRMSE $lt.eq 0.10$, surface correlation $gt.eq 0.99$, and pose-explainable displacement energy $lt.eq 0.80$. Displacement statistics use a five-pixel stride.

All 24 frequency--amplitude combinations passed the predefined geometric recovery criteria over the tested range. Figure #ref(<fig:supp_d1c_representative>, supplement: none) shows a representative 4-cycles-per-aperture, $0.25 thin mu upright(m)$-RMS case. This amplitude lies below the recovery limit obtained after adding image formation and optical-flow estimation, showing that the ray-tracing inverse itself is not the limiting step at this spatial frequency.

#figure(image("source/supplement/figs/AL2550_d1c_4cpa_0p25um_recovery_corrected.pdf", width: 96.0%),
