== Complete synthetic image-chain recovery
<complete-synthetic-image-chain-recovery>
The second synthetic test evaluates the complete image-processing chain $ upright("surface") arrow.r upright("ray tracing") arrow.r upright("synthetic images") arrow.r upright("optical flow") arrow.r upright("surface amplitude") . $ The same 2, 4, 8, and 12 cycles-per-aperture family was tested over the six sag amplitudes for two matched camera models and two image-formation tiers. The ideal renderer includes resampling and area integration but omits blur, illumination variation, added noise, and capture quantization. The Farnebäck adapter histogram-matches and normalizes the pair, then converts both tiers to 8-bit images internally. The realistic renderer additionally uses a Gaussian PSF with $sigma = 0.7$ sensor pixels, radial illumination rolloff 0.10, horizontal gradient 0.03, independent Gaussian read noise with $sigma = 0.5$ digital counts on an 8-bit scale, and 8-bit capture quantization. Both tiers use twofold supersampling, inverse resampling, and area integration at the original pixel pitch. The source is a captured reference texture reconstructed by Lanczos interpolation and scaled to 0.90 of full intensity; these are controlled synthetic inputs, not independently calibrated image-formation parameters.

The cameras are an orthographic model with parallel tilted chief rays and a matched finite-origin spherical-ray model; both use the same aperture labels, base surface, and pose. The complete-image test uses $950 times 950$ images at $0.0297 thin upright(m m \/ p i x e l)$, with a 329-pixel-radius support. It estimates the known sinusoid amplitude from relative flow between base-lens and perturbed-lens images, after separately removing affine flow components. The Farnebäck settings are pyramid scale 0.5, five levels, 15-pixel window, ten iterations, polynomial neighborhood five, polynomial $sigma = 1.2$, and Gaussian weighting, with no iterative dense refinement.

Each complete-image mode must have at least 90% common support and a relative-inversion 99th-percentile error $lt.eq 0.01$ pixels. Profiled flow must have gain in $\[ 0.90 \, 1.10 \]$, correlation $gt.eq 0.95$, and NRMSE $lt.eq 0.30$. Recovered sag must have amplitude gain in $\[ 0.90 \, 1.10 \]$, NRMSE $lt.eq 0.10$, and correlation $gt.eq 0.99$ after independent piston/tilt removal on the evaluation aperture. Crosstalk is tested by the ray-only 16-mode inverse; the complete-image test fits one known mode. A frequency--amplitude cell passes only if all four orientation/phase combinations pass every criterion; the reported limit is the smallest passing tested amplitude for which every larger tested amplitude also passes. The discrete grid does not establish a continuous detection threshold. Across two cameras and two tiers, the test contains 384 mode records and 96 cells.

The base seed is 2026. Capture seeds are $2027 + 1000 thin upright(r o u n d) \( 1000 f \) + 10 j + 2 c + q$, where $j$ indexes the amplitude/orientation/phase combination from zero, $c$ indexes the camera, and $q$ indexes the image tier. The ideal renderer adds no stochastic noise. Ray-only recovery uses no random sampling.

The lowest amplitudes satisfying the recovery criteria were $2$, $1$, $0.5$, and $0.25 thin mu upright(m)$ RMS for ideal image formation and $5$, $2$, $1$, and $1 thin mu upright(m)$ RMS for realistic image formation at 2, 4, 8, and 12 cycles per aperture, respectively. The two camera models gave the same limits. Figure #ref(<fig:supp_f1_recovery_floor>, supplement: none) summarizes these results.

#figure(image("source/supplement/figs/AL2550_synthetic_recoverability_floor_publication.pdf", width: 76.0%),
  caption: [
    Synthetic recovery limit for the complete image and optical-flow pipeline. The realistic image model requires larger sag amplitudes than the ideal image model, particularly at 2--8 cycles per aperture. These limits apply to recovery of the known sinusoidal basis and are not experimental surface-resolution limits.
  ]
)
<fig:supp_f1_recovery_floor>

In this test the sinusoidal basis is known and only the perturbation amplitudes are recovered. The resulting frequency--amplitude limits therefore apply to this controlled basis and do not establish general freeform-surface recoverability.

== Cross-representation controls
<cross-representation-controls>
Figure #ref(<fig:supp_cross_representation>, supplement: none) compares ray-only recovery of six independent truth surfaces using a cubic $7 times 7$ B-spline basis (49 surface parameters), a total-degree-8 Chebyshev basis (45 parameters), and a cubic $7 times 7$ NURBS basis (49 parameters, with fixed nonuniform weights). The truths are a directional sinusoidal mixture at 4 and 8 cycles per aperture, a mixed sampled heightmap, higher-resolution B-spline and NURBS surfaces, a degree-10 Chebyshev surface, and a sampled surface with localized fine structure. The truth and recovery models differ in basis or resolution. Each truth has $1 thin mu upright(m)$ RMS sag after piston/tilt removal over the same 95%-radius evaluation disk used above.

The inverse uses three acquisition geometries with fixed finite-origin camera parameters and fits independent in-plane translations and tilts as alignment parameters. Alternating grid samples are used for fitting and held-out ray evaluation. Surface coordinates are normalized to equal RMS sag, the pose subspace is projected out, and a common relative singular-value cutoff of 0.01 is applied to the linearized surface inverse. Each recovered state is then evaluated with the nonlinear ray model. A comparison passes only if surface NRMSE is $lt.eq 0.10$, surface correlation is $gt.eq 0.99$, held-out ray-perturbation NRMSE is $lt.eq 0.10$, and at least 99.9% of held-out support remains valid. Image formation and optical-flow estimation are excluded.

All three bases recover the smooth B-spline and NURBS truths under these criteria; only Chebyshev also recovers the mixed heightmap. None recovers every tested truth. The sinusoidal mixture and localized fine structure have best-approximation NRMSE of approximately 0.83--0.86 even when their sag is supplied directly to the surface fit, showing that the tested bases have insufficient flexibility for these cases. Reconstruction errors exceed these approximation bounds. For the degree-10 Chebyshev truth, the Chebyshev inverse gives held-out ray NRMSE of 0.063 but surface NRMSE of 0.17. Agreement with the displacement field therefore does not guarantee accurate recovery of an arbitrary freeform surface. These scores include extrapolation within the evaluation disk beyond parts of the sampled ray footprint.

